"""
Mandi Modal Price Prediction — training & evaluation pipeline (v2)
=====================================================================
Rewritten from the original train.py to fix a real data-leakage bug and add
the feature/encoding/model/evaluation improvements requested. This script
is NOT executed against real data by the assistant that wrote it — the
training CSV (india_mandi_prices_nationwide_2024_2025.csv) was not present
in the uploaded files, only the model artifacts and this script were. Every
number this script would produce is left for you to generate by running it
with the real data in place; nothing in metrics.json/feature_importance.csv/
the plots has been fabricated to look improved.

Run with:  python3 train.py
Requires the CSV at DATA_PATH (edit if needed) with the same columns as the
original file (State, District Name, Market Name, Commodity, Variety, Grade,
Season, Price Date, Min Price, Max Price, Modal Price (Rs./Quintal), Source).

================================================================================
SECTION 1 — LEAKAGE AUDIT (findings from reading the original train.py)
================================================================================
1. CONFIRMED LEAKAGE (fixed here): the Lag1/Roll3 fallback median was computed
   with `df.groupby('Commodity')[target].transform('median')` on the FULL
   dataframe — train, validation, AND test rows together — before the
   chronological split. That means a training-set row's fallback value was
   silently influenced by validation/test-period prices. Fixed: the fallback
   is now a fixed map computed from TRAIN ROWS ONLY, then applied unchanged
   to train/val/test (see `train_only_fallback_medians()`).

2. NOT LEAKAGE, but worth flagging: the Lag/Roll features are computed on the
   full chronologically-sorted dataframe via `shift(1)` before the split, which
   IS correct and necessary (a validation-period row's lag legitimately needs
   to see actual, already-realized training-period and earlier validation-period
   prices — that's the whole point of a lag feature). The risk here isn't
   which rows are included, it's ORDER: shift/rolling operate on row POSITION
   within each group, not on the actual date value. If two rows in the same
   (State, District, Market, Commodity, Variety, Grade) group share the exact
   same Price Date, shift(1) could quietly blend one same-day row's price into
   another same-day row's "lag" feature. Added `audit_and_dedupe_same_day()` to
   detect this and aggregate to one row per (group, date) before computing any
   lag/rolling feature, so no same-day information can leak across rows.

3. Target encoding (District/Market/Commodity/Variety) was already fit on
   TRAIN ONLY in v1 — correct, no leakage. v2 improves it with count-based
   shrinkage (a rarely-seen market's encoded value is pulled toward the global
   mean instead of memorizing the mean of a handful of training rows), which
   is a generalization fix, not a leakage fix — the 1,362-market / 302-district
   cardinality with many low-count categories is a strong candidate for the
   train->test generalization gap, since Market_TE alone carries 3.5% of
   permutation importance in the original feature_importance.csv.

4. Same-day Min Price / Max Price were already correctly EXCLUDED in v1 (they
   aren't known until that day's trading closes) — confirmed still excluded
   here; see FEATURE_CLASSIFICATION below for the explicit AVAILABLE-BEFORE-
   PREDICTION vs. TARGET-LEAKAGE call on every feature considered.

5. No other target-derived leakage found: State/District/Market/Commodity/
   Variety/Grade are the query itself (always known); Season/Month/Quarter/
   Week/DayOfYear/sin-cos are deterministic functions of the calendar date
   (always known); all new lag/rolling/change/trend features use shift(1)
   BEFORE any aggregation so the current row's own target is never in its own
   feature (verified in `add_lag_rolling_features()` — every rolling call
   operates on a pre-shifted series, never on the raw group series directly).
================================================================================
"""

